# Deferred work

- Partial setup failure leaks Vulkan objects: if `Renderer::new` fails after
  the swapchain exists (e.g. a `Frame::new` call fails), the swapchain and any
  already-created frames are never destroyed, and neither are the pieces of a
  half-built `Frame` or `Swapchain`. Fix with the deletion queue, so every
  created object is registered for destruction as soon as it exists.
- Split depth ranges: one near plane can't serve both a cockpit 1 m away and a
  planet 10,000 km away (reverse-Z depth below ~1e-8 fails the depth test).
  Render the far scene with a large near plane, clear depth, then render
  nearby objects with a small one, in the same frame. Needed once vessels
  exist; until then the near plane follows the camera's altitude.
- Every pipeline is built for the swapchain's color format. If a swapchain
  recreation ever picks a different format (e.g. the window moves to a
  monitor with other surface formats), the pipelines no longer match. Goes
  away once scene rendering targets its own fixed-format HDR image.
- Pack files: unknown nodes are rejected, but unknown properties are not, so a
  misspelled optional property (e.g. a rate) is silently ignored. Reject
  properties a node doesn't define.
- system-solar data: the Sun's and Moon's spin orientation (pole direction,
  prime meridian) is approximate, and the Moon's mean elements are referred to
  the equinox of date (~0.36° offset by 2026). Fix with ephemerides; invisible
  until bodies are textured.
- The client loads shaders from the `base` pack only, before joining, so other
  packs cannot override them yet (part of custom shaders in packs).
- A remote client must have the server's packs installed with matching
  versions; streaming them is part of multiplayer.

# Next steps

- Commit the pack system (core packs/orbit/time/simulation, base and
  system-solar packs in the namespace-first layout, Joined/JoinDenied
  protocol, content loading in the client, shaders task) — left uncommitted
  until the user asks.
- Translation keys (undecided): explicit keys in files following
  `<pack>.description`, `<pack>.<module>.<entry>.<field>` and
  `<pack>.ui.<screen>.<name>`, or keys derived implicitly from ids.
- Asset hosting: publish binary pack resources as a versioned `.tar.zst`
  archive per pack (players open it with 7-Zip 24.01+ or Windows 11; we create
  it with our own tooling) in S3, optionally behind CloudFront for download
  costs. Repository keeps pack text files only; fetch-assets downloads the
  latest release and extracts the binaries into the working tree (first
  release bootstrapped by hand); a release task packs and uploads, later a
  GitHub Action. Upload credentials through fnox.
- Slang ↔ Rust layout check: the `shaders` task also writes
  `slangc -reflection-json` output; a client build script reads it
  (serde_json as a build dependency) and generates offsets and sizes that Rust
  const assertions compare field by field.
- Grow the per-frame material buffer like the object buffer instead of failing
  past a fixed capacity.
- Handle types (ObjectHandle, MaterialHandle, CubeMapHandle) use a named
  `index` field instead of tuple structs.

# Planned features


- Real solar system at scale: bodies generated from real physical and orbital
  parameters. The general shape must be right; fine detail may be procedural.
- Planet textures from the Sol mod (CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0): attribution required,
  no commercial use, derived textures stay under the same license. Keep the
  texture pipeline source-agnostic so they can be swapped for NASA-derived
  data before any commercial release.
- Texture streaming and a VRAM budget: textures are not all preloaded — only
  the mip levels a texture needs at its current on-screen size are resident,
  loaded and evicted in the background, and total usage stays under the budget
  reported by VK_EXT_memory_budget (least recently used evicted first).
  Required before high-resolution texture packs.
- Planet LOD: cube-sphere faces split into quadtrees as the camera approaches;
  each tile has its own local origin to keep f32 precision on the surface.
- Seamless map mode: the map is the live world zoomed out. The camera stays
  centered on the current target (no automatic re-targeting); map overlays
  (orbits, markers) fade in only once zoomed out far enough to need them.
- Camera modes:
  - Debug: creative-style fly camera — WASD horizontal, Space/Shift up/down,
    scroll changes speed, mouse captured.
  - Game: locked on a target — hold right click to capture the cursor and
    orbit, scroll zooms in and out.
- Time control: pause and warp rates limited by situation (in atmosphere, in
  orbit, eventually interstellar). Rewind is out of scope for performance
  reasons; if ever added, debug mode only.
- Game modes:
  - Survival: career with gradual unlocks and a realistic feel.
  - Creative: survival with infinite resources.
  - Debug: cheats and dev tools — edit positions, orbits and other state.
    The mode used to build the game, also available to players.
- Sky grid debug overlay (to think through): right ascension lines every 1h,
  declination lines every 10°, celestial equator and ecliptic highlighted,
  pole markers — drawn procedurally in the skybox shader.
- HDR rendering with auto-exposure and bloom: stars near the Sun become hard
  to see through exposure and glare (not light pollution — space has none);
  also replaces the fixed dot brightness compromise.
- Multiplayer (single player comes first): time warp across players
  (subspaces), latency with client interpolation and prediction, bandwidth
  (bodies on rails need no streaming), streaming a server's packs to
  connecting clients with consent and safety checks.
- Interstellar travel: Voronoi regions assign space to star systems; vessels
  use positions relative to their system, since f64 resolves only ~8 m at
  interstellar distances.
- AI-assisted translation of lang files through a mise task; English is the
  reference language.
- Effects system (plumes, explosions, particles); atmosphere and cloud visuals
  tie into it.
- Custom shaders in packs: packs ship SPIR-V compiled against the shader
  interface published by `base` (shared module, data layouts); shaders declare
  their material properties in a sidecar so materials set them by name, which
  replaces the closed Material enum. Interface changes bump the pack format.
- 3D model formats: glTF 2.0 preferred; support whatever formats the chosen
  open-source models use.
- Simulation step: planets on rails — Keplerian orbits with barycenters, and
  precomputed ephemerides (e.g. JPL DE440) for real bodies, both pure
  functions of time. Vessels use restricted n-body (planets pull vessels, not
  the reverse) limited to significant bodies, with symplectic integrators for
  coasting, adaptive steps for burns and flybys, and Encke's method (integrate
  the deviation from a Kepler orbit) for large steps. A coasting vessel's
  trajectory is computed once and treated as rails until it changes — cheap
  time warp and the map-mode orbit prediction. Any live n-body between bodies
  stays server-authoritative with periodic keyframes, since floating point
  differs across machines.
